Patch: 0001-adler32-null-buffer-before-one-byte-path
Subject: adler32: test for a null buffer before the one-byte fast path
Class: hardening
Ledger: F0001
C-editions-affected: all
Applies-to: zlib 1.4, commit 99623ef7097352da7993ba614161fd6a311e046e
Witness: tests/witness/F0001_adler32_null_buffer_length_one.c
Upstream-status: not submitted

The manual says of adler32(): "If buf is Z_NULL, this function returns the
required initial value for the checksum."  It places no condition on len.

adler32_z() handled a length of 1 before it tested for a null buffer, so a
call with buf == Z_NULL and len == 1 read buf[0] and dereferenced the null
pointer.  This patch moves the null test in front of the one-byte path, so
the function returns the initial value for a null buffer whatever the
length, as the manual says.

Behavior is unchanged for every call that was defined before:

  - With a non-null buffer, the null test fails and the same statements run
    in the same order as before.
  - With a null buffer and len != 1, the function returned 1 before and
    returns 1 now.

The only call whose behavior changes is buf == Z_NULL with len == 1, which
was undefined and now returns 1.

Cost: the one-byte path executes one more comparison.  Every other path
executes the same comparisons as before.

diff --git a/adler32.c b/adler32.c
--- a/adler32.c
+++ b/adler32.c
@@ -66,6 +66,10 @@
     sum2 = (adler >> 16) & 0xffff;
     adler &= 0xffff;
 
+    /* initial Adler-32 value */
+    if (buf == Z_NULL)
+        return 1L;
+
     /* in case user likes doing a byte at a time, keep it fast */
     if (len == 1) {
         adler += buf[0];
@@ -77,10 +81,6 @@
         return adler | (sum2 << 16);
     }
 
-    /* initial Adler-32 value (deferred check for len == 1 speed) */
-    if (buf == Z_NULL)
-        return 1L;
-
     /* in case short lengths are provided, keep it somewhat fast */
     if (len < 16) {
         while (len--) {
